@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+## Findings
+
+1. **High — MODE_B exposes an off-cone own spear.** [perception.js:44](<C:/arcx/games/tether/src/perception.js:44>) always includes `own.spear`, even when it is non-HELD and outside the 120° cone. A probe facing P1 away from an embedded spear at `(8, 0)` still received its state, position, direction, and surface. [perception.test.mjs:42](<C:/arcx/games/tether/test/perception.test.mjs:42>) asserts this behavior, contrary to SPEC. **Fix:** apply the same cone rule to either player’s non-HELD spear before constructing the percept.
+
+2. **High — an agent can reach the hidden world through the shared JavaScript realm.** [headless.mjs:23](<C:/arcx/games/tether/src/headless.mjs:23>) calls agents in the simulation’s realm. In [the probe](<C:/arcx/games/tether/jobs/rv1-sim/scratch/probes.mjs>), P1 replaced `Array.prototype.map`; the next `step()` exposed the live `world.players` array to that hook, including hidden P2 position. Replacing `Math.hypot` can likewise observe offsets used by visibility checks. The percept copy test does not cover this. **Fix:** run agent code in isolated workers or processes and pass cloned percepts and inputs across the boundary.
+
+3. **High — a legal shallow slide can let a spear pass through an obstacle.** [sim.js:96](<C:/arcx/games/tether/src/sim.js:96>) treats displacement below `epsilon` as parallel, allowing repeated tiny penetration of an expanded obstacle boundary. A throw from that position starts just inside the actual obstacle; [sim.js:109](<C:/arcx/games/tether/src/sim.js:109>) rejects the inside start as an entry. The probe moved along A’s west side for 60 steps, threw from `x=-2.99999998`, and found the spear still `OUTBOUND` at `x=-0.9` after crossing A. **Fix:** prevent accumulated player penetration and treat a spear spawned on or inside static geometry as contact at `t=0`. Add a sustained shallow-slide-to-throw test.
+
+4. **Med — the FOV includes points outside 60°.** [perception.js:15](<C:/arcx/games/tether/src/perception.js:15>) adds an absolute `1e-12` allowance. A point at `60° + 1e-13` radians returns visible; a point `1e-13` units directly behind the viewer also returns visible. [perception.test.mjs:8](<C:/arcx/games/tether/test/perception.test.mjs:8>) tests only a much wider outside angle. **Fix:** use a boundary comparison that does not widen the cone by a fixed world-unit amount, and test outside rays at several distances.
+
+5. **Med — replay reports verified samples without checking their logged contents.** [log.js:170](<C:/arcx/games/tether/src/log.js:170>) compares only `step` and `hash`; it ignores sample fields and event records. Changing a logged P2 position to `999` still returned `verifiedSamples: 3`. [log.test.mjs:124](<C:/arcx/games/tether/test/log.test.mjs:124>) tampers only with the hash. **Fix:** compare each sample and event against replayed output, or label hash verification narrowly and add tests that corrupt data fields and events.
+
+6. **Low — the world hash depends on object key insertion order.** [sim.js:346](<C:/arcx/games/tether/src/sim.js:346>) stringifies nested objects. Rebuilding the same position as `{y: 0, x: -5.5}` changed the hash from `062c87adf2f53ea1` to `07bc6a9f10ec1c43`. This can reject semantically identical snapshots or reserialized logs. **Fix:** hash an explicit ordered sequence of scalar fields. The current `Map` use is lookup based; I found no iteration-order dependency.
+
+**Test gaps:** [sim.test.mjs:99](<C:/arcx/games/tether/test/sim.test.mjs:99>) calls its hit test “no tunnelling,” but the spear endpoint is already inside the victim circle, so an endpoint-only implementation would pass. Use a grazing segment whose endpoints are both outside. The committed tests also lack a corner-contact case, a neutralization-and-hit reset step, and a sustained shallow wall slide. Rendering-specific checklist items—private displays, absent markers/audio, and the drawn wedge—cannot be verified from these headless files.
+
+## Verified as correct
+
+All 35 existing tests pass. Additional probes found a corner spear contact at `(-3, 0.25)` embedding at that point; neutralization followed by a hit in the same step emitted `SPEAR_NEUTRALIZED`, `HIT`, then one `RESET` with the score preserved. Returning spears crossed geometry and completed toward a fixed target in the tested cases. A recall target inside an obstacle required manually placing the owner in an invalid position; it did not reveal a separate reachable failure.
